###  http://fairness_ontology.com/ConsistencyScore
<http://fairness_ontology.com/ConsistencyScore> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                         <http://fairness_ontology.com/DistanceBasedFairnessMetric> ,
                                                         <http://fairness_ontology.com/ScientificArtifact> ,
                                                         <https://purl.org/twc/fmo#fairness_metric> ,
                                                         <https://purl.org/twc/fmo#fairness_notion> ;
                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/hasBeenAppliedTo> <http://fairness_ontology.com/Healthcare> ;
                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/isEquivalentTo> <http://fairness_ontology.com/FairnessThroughAwareness> ;
                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/isProposedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2013_ZEMEL> ;
                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/isUsedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2025_WALLER> ;
                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/measures> <http://fairness_ontology.com/FairnessThroughAwareness> ;
                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/refersTo> <http://fairness_ontology.com/Classification> ;
                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/scientificArtifactDescription> """It measures the overall level of disparity in the classification of individuals with respect to similar individuals, using the k nearest neighbours.  To do so, $yNN$ gives a value in the interval [0, 1], and the higher the value, the lower the level of disparity overall: $y_{\\mathrm{NN}} 
= 1 - \\frac{1}{N_k} \\sum_{n}
\\left| \\hat{y}_n - \\sum_{j \\in k\\text{-NN}(x_n)} \\hat{y}_j \\right| $, where $x_n$ is one of the $N$ examples in the training set.""" ;
                                                <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The aggregation potentially masks high levels of disparity for particular individuals. Very similar to Fairness through awareness, but does not require domain knowledge to define 𝑑." .


