                                                                      <https://purl.org/twc/fmo#fairness_notion> ;
                                                             <http://fairness_ontology.com/isMeasuredBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/Variance> ;
                                                             <http://fairness_ontology.com/isProposedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2021_WU> ;
                                                             <http://fairness_ontology.com/scientificArtifactDescription> "The  recommendation is fair for customers if each customer receives recommendation results with the same Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG) value,i.e., $NDCG(u_1) = NDCG(u_2), \\forall u_1, u_2 \\in U$. NDCG is a ranking metric used to evaluate recommendation systems by measuring the quality of the ranked list of recommended items. It assesses a system's ability to place the most relevant items at the top of the list, with higher scores indicating a better ranking. NDCG is calculated by dividing the Discounted Cumulative Gain (DCG) of the actual recommendation list by the DCG of an ideal list, resulting in a score between 0 and 1." .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/FairScore
<http://fairness_ontology.com/FairScore> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                  <http://fairness_ontology.com/ScientificArtifact> ,
                                                  <http://fairness_ontology.com/ScoreBasedFairnessMetric> ,
                                                  <https://purl.org/twc/fmo#fairness_metric> ;
                                         <http://fairness_ontology.com/isProposedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2022_QIAN> ;
                                         <http://fairness_ontology.com/isUsedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2025_RUSSO> ;
                                         <http://fairness_ontology.com/measures> <http://fairness_ontology.com/CounterfactualFairness> ;
                                         <http://fairness_ontology.com/scientificArtifactDescription> """Is a counterfactual fairness metric that measures “the change in model predictions between the original evaluation dataset and the perturbation augmented version”, with a comparatively lower score indicating a fairer model. The fairscore of a classifier is defined as the percentage of predictions that differ when the input is demographically altered. More formally, for a perturber model $f_P$ and a text snippet $x$, let $\\tilde{x} \\sim f_P(x)$ be the demographically altered perturber output. A classifier $f_C$ exhibits bias if, for some input $x$ and its demographically perturbed version $\\tilde{x}$, the predictions differ, i.e.,
\\[
f_C(x) \\neq f_C(\\tilde{x}).
\\]
Given a classifier $f_C$ and an evaluation set $X$, we define the \\emph{fairscore} $F_S$ as
\\[
F_S(f_C, X)
=
\\frac{\\left| \\{ x \\in X \\mid f_C(x) \\neq f_C(\\tilde{x}) \\} \\right|}{|X|}.
\\]""" .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/FairnessThroughAwareness
<http://fairness_ontology.com/FairnessThroughAwareness> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                 <http://fairness_ontology.com/IndividualFairnessNotion> ,
                                                                 <http://fairness_ontology.com/ObservationalFairnessNotion> ,
                                                                 <http://fairness_ontology.com/OutcomeFairnessNotion> ,
                                                                 <http://fairness_ontology.com/ScientificArtifact> ,
                                                                 <https://purl.org/twc/fmo#fairness_notion> ;
                                                        owl:sameAs <http://fairness_ontology.com/IndividualFairness> ;
                                                        <http://fairness_ontology.com/hasBeenAppliedTo> <http://fairness_ontology.com/Education> ,
                                                                                                        <http://fairness_ontology.com/Healthcare> ,
                                                                                                        <http://fairness_ontology.com/LawAndJustice> ;
                                                        <http://fairness_ontology.com/isEquivalentTo> <http://fairness_ontology.com/IndividualFairness> ;
                                                        <http://fairness_ontology.com/isMeasuredBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/ConsistencyScore> ,
                                                                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/IndividualConsistencyScore> ,
                                                                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/LowIndividualConsistencyCount> ,
                                                                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/ProportionalConsistencyScore> ,
                                                                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/RelativeLInfiniteMetric> ,
                                                                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/SimilarityRobustnessOfIndividualConsistency> ,
                                                                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/TotalVariationNorm> ;
                                                        <http://fairness_ontology.com/isProposedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2012_DWORK> ;
                                                        <http://fairness_ontology.com/isUsedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2018_YONA> ,
                                                                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/2019_LAHOTI> ,
                                                                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/2020_YUROCHKIN> ,
                                                                                                <http://fairness_ontology.com/2025_GAO> ;
                                                        <http://fairness_ontology.com/refersTo> <http://fairness_ontology.com/Classification> ;
                                                        <http://fairness_ontology.com/scientificArtifactDescription> """An algorithm is fair if it gives similar predictions to similar individuals
Awareness is based on the Lipschitz condition, which requires that any two individuals 
$x$ and $y$ at distance $d(x,y) \\in [0,1]$ map to distributions $M(x)$ and $M(y)$, respectively, such that the statistical distance between them is at most $d(x,y)$:
\\[
D(M(x_1), M(x_2)) \\le d(x_1, x_2), 
\\quad \\forall x_1, x_2 \\in E .
\\]

Here, $M(x)$ denotes the probability distribution of the output for individual $x$, 
$E$ is a dataset, $D(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ returns the statistical distance between 
$M(x_1)$ and $M(x_2)$, and $d(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ denotes a distance between individuals 
$x_1$ and $x_2$, defined using a pre-specified, domain-dependent notion of similarity.
\\cite{2025_WALLER}""" .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/IndividualFairness

###  http://fairness_ontology.com/FairnessThroughUnawareness
<http://fairness_ontology.com/FairnessThroughUnawareness> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                   <http://fairness_ontology.com/ProceduralFairnessNotion> ,
                                                                   <http://fairness_ontology.com/ScientificArtifact> ,
                                                                   <https://purl.org/twc/fmo#fairness_notion> ;
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/isProposedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2016_GRGIC-HLACA> ;
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/refersTo> <http://fairness_ontology.com/Classification> ;
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/scientificArtifactDescription> "An algorithm is fair as long as any protected attributes A are not explicitly used in the decision-making process. Hence, remove the sensitive features from the training dataset" ;
                                                          <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "not actually a measure, might increase bias instead of mitigating it. Virtually applicable to any domain. Used as low-performance benchmark by several studies" .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/FalseDiscoveryParity
<http://fairness_ontology.com/FalseDiscoveryParity> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                             <http://fairness_ontology.com/GroupFairnessNotion> ,
                                                             <http://fairness_ontology.com/ObservationalFairnessNotion> ,
                                                             <http://fairness_ontology.com/OutcomeFairnessNotion> ,
                                                             <http://fairness_ontology.com/ScientificArtifact> ,
                                                             <http://fairness_ontology.com/SufficiencyFairnessNotion> ,
                                                             <https://purl.org/twc/fmo#fairness_notion> ;
                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/isMeasuredBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/FalseDiscoveryRate> ;
                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/isProposedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2019_CELIS> ;
                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/isUsedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2019_CELISa> ;
                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/refersTo> <http://fairness_ontology.com/Classification> ;
                                                    <http://fairness_ontology.com/scientificArtifactDescription> """it focuses on equality of False Positive Rates (FPR) across privileged and unprivileged groups. I.e., for each group you compute of all the predicted positive, how many were actually negative (predicted bad). The rates should be equal across different groups. Such notion is useful in cases when false prediction results in additional costs, for example, when classifying whether a person has a medical condition or not. It is mathematically defined as: $P(Y = 0, \\hat{Y} = 1, A = a) = P(Y = 0, \\hat{Y} = 1, A = b),
\\quad \\text{i.e.,} \\quad \\mathrm{FDR}(a) = \\mathrm{FDR}(b).$""" .


