###  http://fairness_ontology.com/MinSkewAndMaxSkew
<http://fairness_ontology.com/MinSkewAndMaxSkew> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/AmortizedGranularityMetric> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/GranularityMetric> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/ImpactFocusedMetric> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/ItemFocusedMetric> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/OptimizationObjectMetric> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/RecommendationSystemMetric> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/ScientificArtifact> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/SingleGranularityMetric> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/SubjectMetric> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/TreatmentFocusedMetric> ,
                                                          <http://fairness_ontology.com/UserFocusedMetric> ,
                                                          <https://purl.org/twc/fmo#fairness_metric> ;
                                                 <http://fairness_ontology.com/isProposedBy> <http://fairness_ontology.com/2019_GEYIK> ;
                                                 <http://fairness_ontology.com/measures> <http://fairness_ontology.com/EqualOpportunity> ,
                                                                                         <http://fairness_ontology.com/StatisticalParity> ;
                                                 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The  equal opportunity notion requires that the fraction of qualified candidates (items) that are included in the top ranked results does not depend  on the protected attribute. This can be met by selecting  the desired distribution p_q to be the distribution of the qualified candidates (items) over the protected attribute, while p_\\tau is the actual distribution. As for statistical parity, for  example, in the case of gender, it would require  that the top results always reflect the gender distribution over all  candidates, irrespective of the specific search or recommendation  task. Statistical parity can be met by selecting the desired distribution p_q to be the distribution of all candidates over the protected attribute. 2023_WANG classifies this as a \"calibration\" metric because it compares the actual distribution of the results with p_f desired distribution, which is function o the merit. However, this \"calibration\" term is not connected to the calbration-based metrics as intended by [2024_CATON] because it does not focus on PPVs. Furthermore, \"some measures of calibrated fairness and consistent fairness are interconvertible. Theoretically, for a calibrated fairness measurement, if we set pf to a uniform distribution, then it can become a measurement for consistent fairness. Similarly, for a consistent fairness measurement that contains f (v ), we can set f (v ) to p(v)/p_f(v) , then it become a calibrated fairness measurement.\" [2023_WANG]" .


